install kafka

establishing connection through terminal
1.
old versions- zookeeper start
bin/zookeeper-server-start.sh config/zookeeper.properties

OR,

new versions(KRaft mode)
-> kafka manages its own metadata
-> but for that to work, we must need to format the storage once

bin/kafka-storage.sh format \
  --config config/server.properties \
  --cluster-id $(bin/kafka-storage.sh random-uuid) \
  --standalone

2. kafka broker start (port: 9092)
bin/kafka-server-start.sh config/server.properties

3. topic create
bin/kafka-topics.sh --create --topic driver_location --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 --partitions 3 --replication-factor 1

verify:
list all topics
bin/kafka-topics.sh --list --bootstrap-server localhost:9092
describe a topic
bin/kafka-topics.sh --describe --topic driver_location --bootstrap-server localhost:9092

4. producer start:
bin/kafka-console-producer.sh --topic driver_location --bootstrap-server localhost:9092

5. consumer start:
bin/kafka-console-consumer.sh --topic driver_location --from-beginning --bootstrap-server localhost:9092

======================================================================================

establishing connection through service

1. dependency
spring-kafka

2. application.properties
#producer configuration
spring.kafka.producer.bootstrap-servers=localhost:9092
spring.kafka.producer.key-serializer=org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer
spring.kafka.producer.value-serializer=org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer

3. topic create
when spring boot app starts & it sees NewTopic bean
spring kafka’s component KafkaAdmin
->automatically connects to Kafka (using spring.kafka.bootstrap-servers property)
->creates the topic with provided configuration if it not exists

4. publish data using KafkaTemplate